--TEST--
`Core\IO\File::flush` hands what was written to the operating system, where a second handle reads it, and a `readLine` that answered `null` answers again once the file has grown — the end of a file is a position and not a latch — spec § 14
--FILE--
<?nvs
// Novis holds no buffer of its own in front of the descriptor, so `flush` is a
// statement about the operating system rather than about this class — and a
// second handle on the same file is where that statement is observable.
Core\IO\File $writer = Core\IO::open("journal.txt", Core\IO\FileMode::Write);
$writer->write("first\n");
$writer->flush();

Core\IO\File $reader = Core\IO::open("journal.txt", Core\IO\FileMode::Read);
echo "1: ", $reader->readLine() ?? "<end>", "\n";
// The reader has caught up with the writer. That is an absence at a position,
// not a state the handle enters.
echo "2: ", $reader->readLine() ?? "<end>", "\n";

$writer->write("second\n");
$writer->flush();
// Same handle, same position, more file: the answer changes because the file
// did, and a program tailing one never has to reopen it.
echo "3: ", $reader->readLine() ?? "<end>", "\n";
echo "4: ", $reader->readLine() ?? "<end>", "\n";

$writer->close();
$reader->close();

// A handle that is gone has nothing left to hand anyone, and asking is a
// mistake in the program rather than a state of the file — which is why every
// member of this class answers it with a `RuntimeError`.
try {
    $writer->flush();
    echo "not reached\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $e) {
    echo "after close: ", $e->message, "\n";
}
--FILE nvs.toml--
[capabilities.fs]
read = ["."]
write = ["."]
--EXPECTF--
1: first
2: <end>
3: second
4: <end>
after close: Core\IO\File::flush: this handle is closed — %s%ejournal.txt
